readonly b.cue
exit 1
rerun

A rerun after a partial write converges to what one run produces. The
first run writes a.cue and fails on b.cue, so the version stays. The
rerun finds a.cue in the target's form: it no longer parses at the old
version, but it parses at the target, so it joins its package as it is,
and the sanitizer sees the field named self it declares; b.cue is then
rewritten with __self, as a one-shot run would write it, and the version
is bumped.

-- cue.mod/module.cue --
module: "mod.test"

language: version: "v0.15.0"

-- a.cue --
package x

self: a: 99
Y=z: {c: Y.d, d: 1}
-- b.cue --
package x

foo: X={i: X.a, a: 1}
-- expect/stderr --
b.cue: write: permission denied
mod upgrade: 1 file(s) could not be written; language version left at v0.15.0
-- expect/a.cue --
package x

self: a: 99
z~(Y): {c: Y.d, d: 1}
-- expect2/stderr --
-- expect2/a.cue --
package x

self: a: 99
z~(Y): {c: Y.d, d: 1}
-- expect2/cue.mod/module.cue --
module: "mod.test"
language: version: "v0.18.0"
-- expect2/b.cue --
package x

foo: {let X = __self, i: X.a, a: 1}
